## 1. Analysis of the sample PDF format (treat as ground truth)

The sample is "SSC CGL Exam Day Based Paper (01 Oct 2026)", published as a text-based PDF (not scanned), 100 questions. Observed structure:

**Page layout**
- Every content page has a header: publisher logo (left), "GET IT ON Google Play" badge (right), a thin horizontal rule, and a faint diagonal watermark behind the text. Page 1 additionally has a blue title bar: `SSC CGL Exam Day Based Paper (01 Oct 2026)`.
- **Full-page advertisement pages** (images only, no extractable text, e.g. "ALL EXAMS, ONE SUBSCRIPTION", "SUCCESS GALLERY", "PREVIOUS YEAR PAPERS PDF") are inserted between content pages. They must be detected (page has ~no text) and skipped.
- The last page has only Q100 and a watermark.

**Question grammar** (text pages)
- Question starts with a bold `Q<number>.` e.g. `Q12. A, B, C, D, E...`
- Options are on separate lines: `(a) ...`, `(b) ...`, `(c) ...`, `(d) ...`
- The correct answer is a bold line right after the options: `Ans.(b)`
- There is no explanation/solution text.
- Questions can have multi-line stems, sub-statements (`1.`, `2.`, `I.`, `II.`, `III.`), passage text, or tables.

**Pitfalls the parser must handle**
- **Page-break splits:** a question's `Ans.(x)` line can land at the top of the next page, right under the header (e.g. Q7's answer, Q22's, Q62's, Q66's). Treat the document as one continuous stream ordered by (page, y) after removing headers/footers/ads.
- **Figure-based questions:** the stem and/or the options are embedded images. In text extraction these appear as empty options like `(a) ` with nothing after them. In the sample: Q1 (mirror image), Q3 and Q9 (figure series), Q13 and Q24 (figure analogy), Q14 (cube from unfolded net). Q24-style items have images in the stem and options.
- **Math-formatted questions:** fractions, roots, superscripts get shredded by text extraction (e.g. Q52, Q53, Q55 come out as `cos x = 12` / `13` on separate lines with the numerator and denominator split across lines). These must be rendered as an **image crop** rather than trusted as text.
- **Tables as text** (Q21 matrices, Q56 data table) come out as flattened rows. Detect tables and render the stem as an image crop (or as a real HTML table if reliably detected).
- **Hyphenation/line-join artefacts** like `longterm`, `postconstitutional` (a hyphen lost at a line break). Provide a de-hyphenation step: if a line ends with `-` join without space; keep a small user-editable fix dictionary.
- **Truncated or odd option text** exists in the source (e.g. Q11(c) `(SU,MI,G`). Never crash; keep the text and add a warning to the report.
- **Passage-based sets:** "Directions (77-81): Read the passage..." precedes Q77–Q81. The passage paragraph must be captured once and attached to all questions in that range (`passage_id`). Detect with regex `Directions\s*\((\d+)\s*-\s*(\d+)\)`.
- Typographic characters: curly quotes, `₹`, `²`, `³`, `√`, `θ`, `€`, `%`, `?` used as code symbols, etc. Preserve Unicode (UTF-8 everywhere).
- The answer key is the publisher's and **may contain mistakes** (some GK items refer to future/unverifiable events, some are debatable). Therefore the app needs a "Report wrong answer / flag question" button and an `overrides` mechanism (Section 3.6).

**Sections inferred from numbering** (25 questions each, matching a 4 × 15 min = 60 min test):
| Q range | Section |
|---|---|
| 1–25 | General Intelligence & Reasoning |
| 26–50 | General Awareness |
| 51–75 | Quantitative Aptitude |
| 76–100 | English Comprehension |

Section boundaries must be **configurable per exam profile** (YAML), because other PDFs may have different sizes/sections.

